        "content": {
          "instructions": "Read each question carefully. Use your knowledge of fractions to complete the tasks. Label parts of fractions, explain scaling relationships, and show your work.",
          "items": [
            "Label the numerator and denominator in these fractions: 2/5, 3/8, 1/2",
            "Explain why 4/6 is equivalent to 2/3. Draw a visual model to show this.",
            "A student says 1/2 is the same as 2/4. Is this correct? Use words and a diagram to justify your answer.",
            "Write two equivalent fractions for 3/4 by scaling. Show how the numerator and denominator change.",
            "Circle the fraction that shows 3 parts out of 5 total: 3/5, 5/3, 2/5",
            "Draw a shape divided into 6 equal parts. Shade 4 parts and write the fraction. Then write an equivalent fraction with denominator 12."
          ],
          "answer_key": [
            "Numerator: top number (2, 3, 1); Denominator: bottom number (5, 8, 2)",
            "Yes, 4/6 simplifies to 2/3 by dividing numerator and denominator by 2. [Visual model showing 4 out of 6 parts shaded and 2 out of 3 parts shaded]",
            "Yes, 1/2 = 2/4 because both represent the same portion. [Diagram showing two identical shapes: one divided into 2 parts with 1 shaded, another into 4 parts with 2 shaded]",
            "Equivalent fractions: 6/8 and 9/12. Numerator and denominator both multiplied by 2 and 3 respectively",
            "3/5",
            "Shaded 4 out of 6 parts. Equivalent fraction: 8/12 (numerator and denominator multiplied by 2)"
          ]
        },
        "estimated_time_minutes": 35,
        "delivery_status": "delivered",
        "delivered_at_cycle": 1
      }
